<article class="env-lemma" id="05CT">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05CT">Lemma <span data-tag="05CT">10.89.11</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. Let $M$ be an $S$-module. If $S$ is Mittag-Leffler as an $R$-module, and $M$ is flat and Mittag-Leffler as an $S$-module, then $M$ is Mittag-Leffler as an $R$-module. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We deduce this from the characterization of Proposition <a href="/tag/059M" data-tag="059M">10.89.5</a>. Namely, suppose that $Q_\alpha $ is a family of $R$-modules. Consider the composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ M \otimes _ R \prod _\alpha Q_\alpha = M \otimes _ S S \otimes _ R \prod _\alpha Q_\alpha \ar[d] \\  M \otimes _ S \prod _\alpha (S \otimes _ R Q_\alpha ) \ar[d] \\  \prod _\alpha (M \otimes _ S S \otimes _ R Q_\alpha ) = \prod _\alpha (M \otimes _ R Q_\alpha ) }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       The first arrow is injective as $M$ is flat over $S$ and $S$ is Mittag-Leffler over $R$ and the second arrow is injective as $M$ is Mittag-Leffler over $S$. Hence $M$ is Mittag-Leffler over $R$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>